{"id":"f933ead4-d836-4d51-a7b0-017337c5b98d","object":"chat.completion","created":1793331359,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Reading the remaining `logger.py` head/tail and the current edit hashes before making changes.\n\n```python\nprint(read_range(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", 1, 32)[\"content\"])\nprint(\"=====\")\nprint(read_range(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", 174, 260)[\"content\"])\n```","reasoning_content":"I need to implement the changes. Let me check whether logger.py imports environ anywhere, and whether there's a `_setup_logger` call. Already saw. The search returned no matches for \"environ\" in logger.py — so I need to import envs in logger.py. Check for circular imports: environ.py imports only stdlib, so fine.\n\nNow implement:\n1. environ.py: add `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_ge32()` — but need validation >=32 and ValueError. Better use lambda:\n\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(_init_log_max_message_chars)\n```\n\nBut descriptors defined at class body time; the factory lambda defined outside the class. Existing pattern uses module-level helpers `_int` etc. I can add a module-level function:\n\n```python\ndef _int_or_none_ge(v: str | None, minimum: int) -> int | None:\n    if v is None:\n        return None\n    n = int(v)  # ValueError with message \"invalid literal for int()...\"\n    if n < minimum:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n    return n\n```\n\nThen in class: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(lambda x: _int_or_none_ge(x, 32))`. But error text must contain variable name — the descriptor wraps errors with f\"{self.name}: {e}\". Good.\n\nWait, empty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError → wrapped. Good.\n\nBut careful with the __get__ error re-raise trick: `raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")`. For ValueError, fine.\n\nNote the descriptor name: `__set_name__` sets name from attribute; descriptor is defined as EnvVar instance in class body, so name set properly.\n\nHmm — but also caching: `owner.__dict__.get(self.name, self)`. Note the lambda creates a new EnvVar each access of class body? No, class body runs once.\n\n2. logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with max_message_chars keyword-only param.\n\n```python\nclass NewLineFormatter(logging.Formatter):\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < _MIN_MESSAGE_BUDGET:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nShould other non-int types (e.g. str) raise ValueError too? Spec: \"其他值须为大于等于32的整数，布尔值不算整数，否则抛出 ValueError\". Yes ValueError for any invalid.\n\nThen in format: need to compute the truncated message. Steps:\n- Use `record.getMessage()` (does %s substitution) — but careful: super().format(record) also uses getMessage and sets record.message. Requirement: budget only message body, not prefix, exception traceback or stack_info.\n\nApproach:\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        truncate = False\n        if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > budget:\n                truncate = True\n        if not truncate:\n            return self._format_message(record)   # original path\n        # else: temporarily substitute a truncated message via a copy of record\n```\n\nTo avoid mutating the shared record's msg/args, create a copy: `record = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)`? That copies attributes; but subtlety: makeLogRecord creates a new LogRecord via `dict` then `_logRecordFactory`... Actually `logging.makeLogRecord(dict)` returns `rv = _logRecordFactory(None, None, \"\", 0, \"\", (), None, None); rv.__dict__.update(dict)`. That works and keeps exc_info etc. However is copying acceptable? It doesn't mutate the original. But be careful: `record.message` may already be set? We compute from getMessage.\n\nAlternative approach: keep original record but wrap: create a shallow copy object via `copy.copy(record)`. `copy` module is stdlib and importable. logging.makeLogRecord is simpler and doesn't require import.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: after copying, need `record.msg = truncated; record.args = None` on the copy only. Also delete `message` if set? The copy has `message` attribute if the original was already formatted by another handler — that would be stale full message. `logging.Formatter.format` uses `record.message = record.getMessage()` in `formatMessage`? Actually in `format()`: `record.message = record.getMessage()` and then `s = self.formatMessage(record)`. So it recomputes. Good—it overwrites message.\n\nThen formatting: super().format(record_copy) with the truncated msg; then multi-line prefix alignment uses `record.message`, which equals truncated msg. Hmm, the original code uses `record` — after reassigning local variable record to copy, `record.message` refers to copy's message. Fine.\n\nAlso color logic uses record.levelno — the copy retains it. Exception traceback appended by super().format via formatException using exc_info — copy retains exc_info so traceback still shown. And stack_info retained. Good: spec says exception traceback not included in budget.\n\nBut careful about \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\". Copy approach satisfies.\n\nActually do we even need the copy? We could avoid copying by building format manually... simpler to copy whole record. But copying for large messages is fine.\n\nHmm, but does copying break `record.created` etc.? No, all in __dict__.\n\nAlternatively avoid copy when not truncating (common path). Good.\n\nAlso careful: `logging.makeLogRecord` may call _logRecordFactory which subclasses might alter; acceptable.\n\nLet me instead use a small helper to compute truncated message:\n\n```python\n_OMIT_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n_MIN_MESSAGE_BUDGET = 32\n\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, budget: int) -> str:\n    marker = _OMIT_MARKER\n    remaining = budget - len(marker)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return message[:budget]\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return f\"{message[:head]}{marker}{message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else ''}\"\n```\n\nCheck example: budget 32, marker len 15, remaining 17, head = 9, tail = 8. Matches \"预算32时，保留开头9字符、末尾8字符\". Good.\n\nEdge: tail 0 → message[-0:] is whole string, so guard.\n\nResult length: head + 15 + tail = 32 = budget. Good. Note if budget < len(marker) we return marker... but budget>=32 always so fine; still safe fallback: return marker[:budget].\n\nAlso need to consider that we truncate before computing... The point 2: \"先完成 %s 等参数替换，再计算消息长度\" — getMessage does that.\n\nDefault init: `_setup_logger`:\n```python\n    fmt = NewLineFormatter(\n        _FORMAT,\n        datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT,\n        use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(),\n        max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS,\n    )\n```\n\nBut careful: `_setup_logger` is called at import? Let me check where it is called and whether reading envs at import may raise ValueError (invalid env during import would break). Test likely sets env then imports. If env invalid, ValueError at setup — acceptable? Spec says access raises ValueError. Hmm, but if user's env invalid, import fails. That's per spec presumably acceptable.\n\nHowever there's risk: tests may create formatter and test env reading separately. Also test may check \"重复初始化不能重复添加处理器\" — unchanged.\n\nImportant risk: `_setup_logger` called at module import time in logger.py (probably `_setup_logger()` at bottom). If test sets env var after import and calls `_setup_logger()` again, it should re-read the config value — but envs config caches on first access. In _setup_logger we access `envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`, which caches on first access. Scenario in spec: read 32, change env to 64, re-init logger still uses 32; rearm then re-init uses 64. That works because config cached.\n\nBut: is the config accessed at import time (when _setup_logger runs at import)? Then the first cached read happens at import, before tests set env var → tests setting env var later would get cached None! Test likely: set env 64, import/reload logger? Hmm, \"课程测试会自行设置它\" and the integration check \"实际加载工作副本的配置、日志模块\". The test likely sets env var, then calls rearm/rearm_all, then calls _setup_logger. Or it might reload modules. Risky.\n\nLet me look at logger.py fully to see the import-time call, and how environ is used elsewhere (e.g. other modules read envs and it caches at import). Existing pattern: other modules call `envs.DEEPINFER_...` at use time. Tests for such courses typically do: os.environ[\"X\"]=\"64\"; importlib.reload or envs-rearm.\n\nI should read the whole logger.py to see the structure (line count ~250?). Let me read the beginning lines 1-35 to see imports and bottom lines.\n\nAlso should read stats_logger.py lines around 100-135 (already have _log_worker with logger.info(m)) → change to `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nNow let's do the reads needed: logger.py 1-32, and 174-end; stats_logger.py full already registered; environ.py full read registered.\n\nLet me now check logger.py head for imports and __all__ / bottom.\n\nAlso note: initial full reads are registered, so I can read ranges freely. Let me fetch needed ranges to edit precisely."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10025,"completion_tokens